§ `Bracketing the operand does not narrow NOT (SET-OPERATORS-SPEC §18.1, R-NOT-1)`

-- The closing bracket ends `a`; it does not end the NOT's reach, so these
-- still read as NOT (a AND b) and are refused for the same reason as the
-- unbracketed spelling. The form that narrows is (NOT a) AND b, with the
-- bracket closing around the NOT itself. NOT-REACH-OK is for the text
-- checker, which sweeps this directory too.

-- the form that looks maximally careful, and is exactly as wide
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
looksCareful a b MEANS NOT (a) AND b -- NOT-REACH-OK: the refused spelling, on purpose

-- a bracketed group followed by a connective on the NOT's line: the group
-- is one thing, and the AND after it is still reached over
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
groupThenAnd a b MEANS NOT (a OR b) AND b -- NOT-REACH-OK

-- a bracketed call, likewise
GIVEN x IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
flip x MEANS NOT x

GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN, b IS A BOOLEAN
callThenOr a b MEANS NOT (flip a) OR b -- NOT-REACH-OK
